# Caddy's own logging, imported into the global block of both the host's
# Caddyfile and the allowlist test's, so the test covers what the host runs
# (ADR-0022 item 14).
#
# Having no `log` directive in sites.caddy turns off the access log only.
# Caddy still logs every request a handler fails, a 502 from a restarting API
# or Keycloak included, under `http.log.error`, and that entry carries the
# request: the client's address, the full URI with its query string (the OIDC
# `?code=` and `state`, a sync cursor), and the User-Agent. Every deploy and
# every outage would write one per request in flight. An address tied to this
# app is health information under the FTC rule.
#
# So the default logger excludes it, and `http.handlers.reverse_proxy` with it,
# whose entries carry the same request. What remains is Caddy's startup,
# certificate and configuration messages, which name no client. That an
# upstream is down is the readiness endpoint's and slice 5's monitoring's to
# report, not a log line per patient request.

log default {
	output stderr
	format json
	exclude http.log.error http.handlers.reverse_proxy
}
